 **KERNEL-66 amendment (2026-10-24, FIX-KERNEL; lang-journey was RED on main since f1839ed).** Three journey rows expected the old order. LB8b (a second answer of one ring over the view the first answer moved) expected the guard's `rung: answered`; LH7b (a select over the view before a re-pin) expected the method's `support0 moved`; P8.2 (a plan signed before the revoke of its provider's grant) accepted either `support0 dead` or `rejected stale`. Each call's pin moved, so under KERNEL-66 each is `rejected stale`, declined before the guard or method runs. That is the name KERNEL-66 means: the refusal names the stale observation, not what a receiver says of the new state. The rows now expect exactly `rejected stale`; LB8 and LH7 still read the guard's and the method's own refusals on a current view. MEASURED (hbox wt/fixkernel on 0a299c8): lang-journey GREEN in both modes. THE BAR (`--records 60 --standing`) reads PASS, and its `--harness-control` reads RED harness-control with every comparator fired, so no `refused stale` appears (FIX-PROTOCOL's BAR line was INFERRED; it does not reproduce). Its expected word is `declined stale` (scripts/bar.sh `STALE`), unchanged. A different defect found while reading: `Protocol.Command.staleBehind` (Machine.lean) still classifies a call stale by the OLD order (`receive`, then `World.admit`'s `stale`). On follow's World, a call whose pin moved but whose receiver also refuses on the new state reads `false`, so its settlement stays `open` where the Host now declines it `stale`. Its twin-free form is `checkPins` on follow's World. `staleBehind_spec` and `Behind.lean`'s finality proofs restate with it. This is PROTOCOL's file; it is handed to FIX-PROTOCOL as a card.
+
+## KERNEL-68 · The meter: a Turn names its member and its ticks, and the kernel counts a member's ticks per window as it counts refusals (FABLE-OBS, 2026-10-27; DESIGN-OBS §1, redregg/work/promo/DESIGN-OBS.md; FRONTIER-AXES axis 4)
+
+**Decision.**
+1. `Turn` gains `member : D` and `ticks : Nat` (`Kernel/Turn.lean`): who signed the call, and the ticks
+   its whole tree of runs used (0 for every Body but an invoke). `Receive.sealTurn` sets both from the
+   call and the Walk's pool (`deriveWithTicks`); every follower re-derives them from the event
+   (`Receive.rederive`: a page carries the call, never the writer's Turn), so a writer that states another
+   member or count is `eventMismatch`. The log record grows by 40 bytes (`logWeight`; `Codec.Kernel.turn`
+   gains the two fields); `checkBounds` refuses `ticks` past `treeTicksMax` (`overBound .ticks`): a forged
+   log cannot jump the meter.
+2. The refusals trie gains a fourth key kind, `RefusalSlot.ticks member win` (kind byte 3, one width with
+   the others), holding the existing `count` leaf: the member's admitted ticks in the window the Turn
+   lands in (`refusalWindow (height + 1)`, the journal's window). `World.admit` checks `ticksUsed +
+   t.ticks ≤ Bounds.ticksPerWindow` (`checkTicks`, beside `checkWindow`; refused `ticksBudget member`,
+   a class-(A) rejection `judge` records as it records `overAllowance`) and, for a Turn with ticks, writes
+   the leaf (`commitMeter`: one trie path per invoke, none for any other Body) before the window prune.
+   `refusalFits` bounds the leaf (1 ≤ n ≤ `ticksPerWindow`, the current window); `keptRefusal` prunes it
+   with the counts; `refusalsCounted` accepts it (the Turns that summed it are not in the trie: see critique).
+3. `Bounds.ticksPerWindow := 16 * treeTicksMax` (2^21): sixteen full-budget trees per member per window,
+   by analogy with `refusalsPerWindow = 16`; ~70,000 invokes of THE BAR's size.
+4. Probes and rows: kernel-sim `probeTicks` (sixteen full trees admitted on a copy, the seventeenth
+   `ticksBudget`, a tree past `treeTicksMax` `overBound`; planted names `ticksBudget`,
+   `ticks-window:ticksBudget`); lang-journey M0 (B's meter reads back as the sum of the ticks of B's
+   admitted Turns in the session log, and A's founder has none).
+
+**Why.** A member may spend `treeTicksMax` ticks per call at every height it can get a call into, bounded
+by nothing (READ `Derive.lean` `poolOf`); refusals were given a window budget (KERNEL-44) and admissions
+were not. The honest self-measurements of a World are the functions of its log the kernel maintains and a
+follower re-derives (DESIGN-OBS §1.2); the refusals trie is the one place the kernel already keeps a
+per-window per-member counter under the chain, pruned on the schedule a meter wants, and readable by a
+path against the refusals root. A kernel-owned CELL would be touched by a Turn that did not name it
+(`admit_frame`, `fold_potential`, every pin theorem) and a receiver-written one would serialise a member's
+calls on its pin (KERNEL-54's reason refusal counts are not pins). A fifth trie is the cut HOLISTIC-KERNEL
+§2.2 judged wrong. The member in the Turn rather than recovered from the event: the kernel never reads the
+event; a decoder in `Env` would sit in `World.admit`'s hot path and in every kernel theorem's premises;
+`Refused` carries `member` for the same reason (KERNEL-50).
+
+**Alternatives.** (a) Bytes per member per window (Σ `charge`) first: cheaper and computed by the kernel
+with no receive-layer trust; declined as the FIRST meter because bytes already have a global budget
+(`allowance`) and ticks have none; it is the second key kind, same leaf, same check (DESIGN-OBS §1.4).
+(b) A `ticks` field in the journal entry so the meter is a tally of the journal (KERNEL-50's property for
+refusals): restates the journal leaf, its injectivity and every `JournalHead` theorem, for a check whose
+only reader is a base decode. Declined. (c) A member only on invoke Turns (`Option D`): a forged-log probe
+shape on the wire. Declined; every Turn naming its signer is what B1 (authority confinement) wants.
+
+**Price.** Hypotheses on landed theorems: 0 (a new key kind in an existing trie). Restated by binder: the
+26 Turn construction sites (MEASURED), `Codec.Kernel.turn` and its size/injectivity theorems, the refusals
+families that case on the slot (`refusalFits`, `keptRefusal`, `RefusalLeaf.fits`, `refusalKeyBytes`,
+`refusalsCounted`, `RefusalsBound`, `RefusalsHead`, Codec/World's slot codec and `WorldSize`). Flag day
+`kernel!`: the Turn record and the refusal code table (`rejectNames` + `ticksBudget`) move, so every
+pre-meter Store is `shapeMismatch` and re-geneses; `codec-vectors` re-emits. Waste: `admit
+trie-hashes-per-turn` rises by one path for invoke Turns only (the sim's `Required` counts it through
+`fresh`); `submit bytes-per-turn` falls (40 bytes in numerator and denominator).
+
+**Refuses.** `ticksBudget MEMBER` (recorded, final within the window), `overBound ticks`, `shapeMismatch`
+for a pre-meter Store, `kernel/refusals` for a ticks leaf outside its window or past `ticksPerWindow`.
+
+**CRITIQUE.** (1) `refusalsCounted` cannot re-tally a ticks leaf from the trie (the Turns are not in it), so
+a decoded base with a forged meter passes the state check and is caught only by the trust a received base
+already needs (`CheckpointTrusted`, a sponsor's pin). KERNEL-50's "a function of its records" holds for
+refusals and not for ticks; the fold theorem `fold_ticks_meter` (NOT yet written: the next step) is its
+truth. (2) The cap is a taste number with no OWNER: a member cannot buy or be granted more. If a row (the
+Bell's resident, an agent member) approaches 2^21 ticks in a window, the honest growth is `Right.ticks cap`
+as a counted right carved from a founder's World grant, spent per invoke like `enroll n`. (3) 32 bytes of
+member on every record for a counter only invokes need.
+
+**META-CRITIQUE.** The refusals trie was chosen because KERNEL-44 built it and it is where the kernel already
+measures itself; its name is now wrong (it is the WINDOW trie) and this entry does not rename it. The brief
+said "ticks per window per member is the obvious one"; the bytes meter is cheaper and more honest and was
+nearly built first; ticks won only because it is the hole with no cousin.
+
+**Status.** built in `wt/fable-obs`; landing as `kernel!` (FABLE-OBS.report.md holds the hashes).
